      "text": "The ball starts at $243$ cm tall and bounces back up to $\\frac{2}{3}$ of that height at each bounce. We need to determine how many bounces it takes for the height of the bounce to be less than $30$ cm, and list all the critical height values of the bounce.\n\n\n1. First bounce height: $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{2}= \\frac{4}{9} \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n2. Second bounce height: $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{3}= \\frac{8}{27} \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n3. Third bounce height: $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{4}= \\frac{16}{81} \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n4. Fourth bounce height: $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{5}= \\frac{32}{243} \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n5. Fifth bounce height: $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{6}= \\frac{64}{729} \\mathrm{~cm}$.\n\nAt this point, the height of the bounce is $\\frac{64}{729}$ cm, which is less than $30$ cm. Therefore, the ball first rises to a height less than $30$ cm $\\boxed{5}$ times during its initial fall.\n\nConsider an arithmetic sequence with 2019 terms. The sum of all its terms is 1011, and the sum of every four consecutive terms is even. Find the middle term.\nLet the arithmetic sequence be \\(\\{a_n\\}\\) with the first term \\(a\\) and common difference \\(d\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence can be expressed as \\(a_n = a + (n-1)d\\), and the sum of the first 2019 terms is given by\n\\[\nS_{2019} = \\frac{2019}{2} \\left(2a + 2018d\\right) = 1011.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to\n\\[\n2019a + 2019 \\cdot 1019d = 1011 \\implies 2019(a + 1019d) = 1011 \\implies a + 1019d = 0 \\implies a = -1019d.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the sum of every four consecutive terms. The sum of every four consecutive terms is\n\\[\n(a + 3d) + (a + 4d) + (a + 5d) + (a + 6d) = 4a + 19d = 4a + 19d.\n\\]\nGiven that this sum is even for each set of four consecutive terms, we have\n\\[\n4a + 19d \\text{ is even.} \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n\\]\n\nWe substitute \\(a = -1019d\\) into Equation 1:\n\\[\n4(-1019d) + 19d = -4076d + 19d = -4057d.\n\\]\nFor the above expression to be even, \\(d\\) must be odd. Let \\(d = 2k + 1\\) for some integer \\(k\\) (i.e., \\(d\\) is an odd number).\n\nSubstituting \\(d = 2k + 1\\) into \\(a = -1019d\\), we get\n\\[\na = -1019(2k+1) = -2038k - 1019.\n\\]\n\nThus, the middle term of the arithmetic sequence, which is the 1010th term, can be expressed as\n\\[\na_{1010} = a + 1009d = -2038k - 1019 + 1009(2k + 1).\n\\]\nSimplify this expression:\n\\[\na_{1010} = -2038k - 1019 + 2018k + 1009 = -10k - 10.\n\\]\nThus, the middle term is\n\\[\na_{1010} = -10k - 10.\n\\]\nGiven that \\(k\\) is an integer, we have \\(d = 2k + 1\\) and the middle term is always a specific linear function of \\(k\\):\n\\[\n\\boxed{-10k - 10}.\n\\]\n\nIf $\\tan\\theta=2$, then $\\frac{\\sin \\theta+\\cos \\theta}{2\\sin \\theta -3\\cos \\theta}=$ _____ .\n\nGiven that \\(\\tan\\theta = 2\\), we need to find the value of \\(\\frac{\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta}{2\\sin\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta}\\).\n\nFirst, recall that \\(\\tan\\theta = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = 2\\). This implies that \\(\\sin\\theta = 2\\cos\\theta\\). We can substitute \\(\\sin\\theta\\) with \\(2\\cos\\theta\\) in the expression we need to evaluate.\n\nThe expression \\(\\frac{\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta}{2\\sin\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta}\\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\cos\\theta + \\cos\\theta}{2(2\\cos\\theta) - 3\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{3\\cos\\theta}{4\\cos\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{3\\cos\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = 3.\n\\]\n\nSo, the value is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).\n\nA ball rolls down a hill and then back up the other side, reaching a maximum height that is 25% of the way up the original hill. If the ball starts and ends at the same level, and it takes 10 seconds to reach the top and back, how long does it take the ball to reach the highest point during its one-way journey?\n\nTo determine how long it takes the ball to reach the highest point during its one-way journey, we need to break down the problem into manageable parts.\n\nFirst, let's summarize the entire journey:\n1. The ball travels down the hill to a maximum height that is 25% of the original height.\n2. The ball then rolls back up to the original height on the hill.\n3. The ball takes 10 seconds to reach the top and back to the original height.\n\nSince the ball takes 10 seconds to reach the top and back, and it starts and ends at the same level, the time taken to reach the highest point is half of the total time to travel both ways. This is because the time taken to travel down the hill is the same as the time taken to travel back up the hill in the reverse direction.\n\nTherefore, the time taken to reach the highest point is:\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\text{ seconds}}{2} = 5 \\text{ seconds}\n\\]\n\nSo, the time it takes the ball to reach the highest point during its one-way journey is \\(\\boxed{5}\\) seconds.\n\nIn the range $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$ with the constraint that $\\cos{x} + \\cos{2x} + \\cos{4x} + \\cos{8x} + \\cos{14x} = 0$, find the sum of the cosines for the cosine terms. \n\nA) $\\cos 8x$\n\nB) $1$\n\nC) $0$\n\nD) $\\cos 4x$\n\nE) None of these\n\nTo solve the given equation \\(\\cos{x} + \\cos{2x} + \\cos{4x} + \\cos{8x} + \\cos{14x} = 0\\) in the range \\(0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi\\), we start by considering the sum of cosines of the form \\(\\cos{2kx}\\) for \\(k = 1, 2, 4, 8, 14\\).\n\nThe sum of cosines can be rewritten using the properties of cosine functions. Recall the identity for the sum of cosines of multiple angles. Specifically, for the sum of cosines of angles that are multiples of \\(x\\) in a range where the values sum to zero, we can use the fact that the sum of cosines of these angles is zero. This is a well-known property in trigonometry.\n\nIn our case, the angles are \\(x\\), \\(2x\\), \\(4x\\), \\(8x\\), and \\(14x\\). If we consider the sum of these cosines, which is \\(\\cos{0} + \\cos{2x} + \\cos{4x} + \\cos{8x} + \\cos{14x}\\), we notice that the sum of these cosines is zero.\n\nHowever, the problem asks for the sum of the individual cosines of the terms, so we need to determine which cosine term is zero in the given range. The only term that is zero in the given range is \\(\\cos{0}\\), which is simply 1.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the cosines for the cosine terms",
