      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that the ball bounces back up to $\\frac{2}{3}$ of its previous height, the height after each bounce can be modeled as a geometric progression with a common ratio of $\\frac{2}{3}$. \n\nWe know the initial height of the ball is $243$ cm. We want to determine when the height after bounces becomes less than $30$ cm. That is, we want to find the smallest $n$ for which $243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < 30$.\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality $243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < 30$. We do this by solving for $n$:\n\n1. Move all terms to one side of the inequality to set it to $0$.\n\\[ 243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n - 30 < 0 \\]\n\n2. This is of the form $m - kn < 0$, where $m=243$ and $k=30$. Thus, since $\\frac{2}{3}$ is less than $1$, the only way this can happen is if $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < 1$. Taking the natural logarithm on both sides and dividing by the natural logarithm of $\\frac{2}{3}$, we find:\n\n\\[ n \\cdot (\\ln \\frac{2}{3}) < \\ln \\left(\\frac{30}{243}\\right) \\]\n\n\\[ n > \\frac{\\ln \\left(\\frac{243}{30}\\right)}{\\ln \\frac{2}{3}} \\]\n\n3. We need to estimate the value of $\\ln \\left(\\frac{243}{30}\\right)$ and $\\ln \\frac{2}{3}$. Using a calculator, we get:\n\n\\[ \\ln \\left(\\frac{243}{30}\\right) \\approx 1.806 \\]\n\n\\[ \\ln \\frac{2}{3} \\approx -0.405 \\]\n\n4. Dividing to get $n$:\n\n\\[ n > \\frac{1.806}{-0.405} \\approx -4.45 \\]\n\n5. Since the number of bounces can't be negative, we round $4.45$ up to $5$.\n\nThus, the ball first gets to height less than $30$ cm after $5$ bounces.",
